From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "D.R.racer" <drracer@seznam.cz>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 15:57:50 +0200
Subject: rtc: sunxi: run LOSC from the external 32k crystal when present

The driver leaves the 32 kHz low-speed oscillator (LOSC) on the
internal RC oscillator. On a Cubietruck, LOSC as seen by the RTC runs
3.2 % slow (about 31.7 kHz) on it, and -28 ppm on the crystal, both
measured against an NTP-synced system clock.

LOSC is also the clock behind CLK_OUT_A, which the Cubietruck feeds to
its AP6210 WiFi/Bluetooth module as the low-power clock. On the RC
oscillator the Bluetooth controller answers every HCI command, but its
radio neither transmits nor receives: LE scans and inquiries return no
reports, and other devices cannot discover it. Switching LOSC to the
crystal at runtime and re-initialising the controller makes it work;
switching back to the RC oscillator broke it again in each of three
tries.

Select the crystal when the device tree provides it, similar to what
the A31 RTC driver does. The value written leaves LOSC_CTRL at 0x9
(crystal, drive strength 2, all other control bits clear), which is
what the vendor 3.4 kernel leaves in the register. The hardware
falls back to the RC oscillator by itself when the crystal stops or
fails to start (LOSC_CTRL bit 13 then reads as set), so the RTC keeps
running, if inaccurately.

Tested on a Cubietruck (A20): from a cold power-on, LOSC_CTRL reads 0x9
right after boot and Bluetooth scans; on the patched kernel it also
pairs and answers l2ping. Boards that do not reference the crystal are
unaffected.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/rtc/rtc-sunxi.c | 20 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/rtc/rtc-sunxi.c b/drivers/rtc/rtc-sunxi.c
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/drivers/rtc/rtc-sunxi.c
+++ b/drivers/rtc/rtc-sunxi.c
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
  * Copyright (c) 2013, Carlo Caione <carlo.caione@gmail.com>
  */
 
+#include <linux/clk.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/err.h>
 #include <linux/fs.h>
@@ -19,8 +20,11 @@
 #include <linux/types.h>
 
 #define SUNXI_LOSC_CTRL				0x0000
+#define SUNXI_LOSC_CTRL_KEY			(0x16aa << 16)
 #define SUNXI_LOSC_CTRL_RTC_HMS_ACC		BIT(8)
 #define SUNXI_LOSC_CTRL_RTC_YMD_ACC		BIT(7)
+#define SUNXI_LOSC_CTRL_EXT_GSM(x)		((x) << 2)	/* 2 bits */
+#define SUNXI_LOSC_CTRL_EXT_OSC			BIT(0)
 
 #define SUNXI_RTC_YMD				0x0004
 
@@ -420,6 +424,7 @@ MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, sunxi_rtc_dt_ids);
 static int sunxi_rtc_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct sunxi_rtc_dev *chip;
+	struct clk *ext_osc;
 	int ret;
 
 	chip = devm_kzalloc(&pdev->dev, sizeof(*chip), GFP_KERNEL);
@@ -453,6 +458,21 @@ static int sunxi_rtc_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		return -ENODEV;
 	}
 
+	ext_osc = devm_clk_get_optional_enabled(&pdev->dev, NULL);
+	if (IS_ERR(ext_osc))
+		return dev_err_probe(&pdev->dev, PTR_ERR(ext_osc),
+				     "Could not get the external oscillator\n");
+
+	/*
+	 * Run LOSC from the external crystal, with the drive strength the
+	 * vendor kernel uses. Like the vendor kernel, this clears the other
+	 * control bits. The hardware still falls back to the RC
+	 * oscillator by itself if the crystal stops.
+	 */
+	if (ext_osc)
+		writel(SUNXI_LOSC_CTRL_KEY | SUNXI_LOSC_CTRL_EXT_GSM(2) |
+		       SUNXI_LOSC_CTRL_EXT_OSC, chip->base + SUNXI_LOSC_CTRL);
+
 	/* clear the alarm count value */
 	writel(0, chip->base + SUNXI_ALRM_DHMS);
 
-- 
